Odd/Even Cycles, Primes, and Doubled Primes — opus-S550

======================================================================
PART 1: Only ODD cycles matter — the OCF structure
======================================================================

n=4 at regular polygon (t=1/4):
  3-cycles:     1  (ODD)
  4-cycles:     0  (even)
  directed 3-cycles (c3): 1

n=5 at regular polygon (t=1/5):
  3-cycles:     2  (ODD)
  4-cycles:     1  (even)
  5-cycles:     0  (ODD)
  directed 3-cycles (c3): 2

n=6 at regular polygon (t=1/6):
  3-cycles:     5  (ODD)
  4-cycles:     5  (even)
  5-cycles:     1  (ODD)
  6-cycles:     0  (even)
  directed 3-cycles (c3): 5

n=7 at regular polygon (t=1/7):
  3-cycles:     8  (ODD)
  4-cycles:    12  (even)
  5-cycles:     6  (ODD)
  6-cycles:     1  (even)
  7-cycles:     0  (ODD)
  directed 3-cycles (c3): 8

ODD CYCLES FACT:
  The OCF uses ONLY odd cycles. Even cycles decompose into pairs.
  A tournament's H(T) is determined by its odd-cycle conflict graph.
  The independence polynomial I(Ω, 2) = Σ 2^k × (# independent k-sets)
  counts collections of VERTEX-DISJOINT ODD cycles.

  WHY ONLY ODD? Because tournaments orient COMPLETE graphs, and the
  parity constraint from the complete graph's even Euler characteristic
  means even cycles decompose: a 4-cycle A→B→C→D→A always contains
  either A→C or C→A as a 'shortcut', creating two 3-cycles.

