    (2,6,4): diffs=(+4,-2,-2) = [2p=4, p=2, p=2]

GOLDBACH-CYCLE CONNECTION:
  Every 3-cycle has three speed differences summing to 0.
  One difference is the SUM of the other two (with signs).
  When two differences are PRIME: the third is EVEN = Goldbach sum.
  The doubled primes (2p) appear as these EVEN differences.

  In the OCF: 3-cycles are the FUNDAMENTAL odd cycles.
  Their weight μ(C) = H(T[V\V(C)]) depends on the complement.
  The Goldbach structure of the differences determines μ(C)
  through the arithmetic of the speed set.

  THE DOUBLED PRIMES ARE THE BRIDGES: they connect two prime
  speed differences into a closed cycle. Without doubled primes,
  many 3-cycles wouldn't exist. They're the 'glue' between
  prime-frequency Gabor atoms.

======================================================================
PART 5: The doubled prime's role — five perspectives
======================================================================

1. P-ADIC TREE: doubled primes = level 1 of the 2-adic tree.
   They sit BETWEEN the primes (level 0) and the 4-multiples (level 2).
   They're the first 'harmonic' above the fundamental prime layer.

2. RESONANCE DEBT: doubled-prime pairs (p, 2p) have speed
   difference p, which creates a resonance at frequency p/n.
   The Bernoulli term B₂(p/n) for these pairs is typically LARGE
   (because p/n is often near 1/2 for moderate primes).

3. OCF / 3-CYCLES: doubled primes bridge prime differences.
   A 3-cycle with runners (a, b, c) where b-a=p (prime) and
   c-b=q (prime) has a-c=-(p+q) (even = Goldbach sum).
   The doubled prime 2r appears when p+q=2r, i.e., the sum
   of two primes equals twice a prime (Goldbach partition).

4. CASCADE: doubled primes are processed at the 2-adic level 1.
   In the cascade descent (S544): after clearing the odd speeds
   (level 0), the doubled primes are the NEXT constraint.
   Their clearance is the '2-adic step' of the cascade.

5. GABOR: doubled primes are the 2nd harmonic of prime atoms.
   In the time-frequency plane (S541): the atom at frequency 2p
   is the OCTAVE of the atom at frequency p.
   The interference between p and 2p creates a 'beat' at frequency p.
   This beat IS the pairwise resonance term in the debt (S531).

THE DEEP POINT:
  Primes are the IRREDUCIBLE frequencies (Gabor atoms).
  Doubled primes are their FIRST HARMONICS (octaves).
  The relationship prime ↔ doubled prime is:
    - Goldbach: the even number = p + q (two primes)
    - Lemoine: the odd number = p + 2q (prime + doubled prime)
    - OCF: the 3-cycle = two prime arcs + one even bridge
    - Resonance: the pairwise term = beat between p and 2p
    - P-adic: the tree level 0 → level 1 transition

  Without doubled primes: the OCF has FEWER 3-cycles,
  the resonance debt is SMALLER, and LRC is EASIER.
  Doubled primes make LRC HARDER by creating additional
  cycle-interference — they're the 'complication' that the
  arithmetic progression exploits for maximum coherence.

  The AP {1,...,n-1} has the MOST doubled primes in range
  (every even number ≤ n-1 is a doubled prime or composite).
  This is another reason AP is the tightest case.

